APS-012 — Policy Inheritance

Document ID: APS-012
Title: Policy Inheritance
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Policy Inheritance Model (CPIM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Policy Inheritance Model establishes the principles, mechanisms, governance, and semantics governing how Canonical Policy Objects inherit, specialize, extend, and override one another while preserving consistency, traceability, and engineering integrity.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Policy inheritance architecture
Inheritance semantics
Specialization
Extension
Override rules
Hierarchical governance
Conflict prevention
Traceability

This specification applies to every Canonical Policy Object participating in inheritance relationships.

3. Canonical Definition

Policy Inheritance is the governed mechanism by which a Canonical Policy Object acquires, specializes, extends, or overrides the behavior of another Canonical Policy Object while preserving hierarchical consistency and governance.

Inheritance enables controlled specialization.

It SHALL NOT duplicate policy semantics.

4. Objectives

The Policy Inheritance Model SHALL:

Enable policy reuse.
Preserve governance hierarchy.
Reduce policy duplication.
Support specialization.
Preserve traceability.
Prevent contradictory inheritance.
Support federation.
Enable scalable governance.
5. Inheritance Principles

Every inheritance relationship SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Hierarchical Consistency

Child policies SHALL remain consistent with inherited mandatory semantics.

Explicit Inheritance

Inheritance SHALL be explicitly declared.

Implicit inheritance SHALL NOT exist.

Traceability

Inherited relationships SHALL remain permanently traceable.

Reusability

Inheritance SHALL maximize policy reuse.

Governed Specialization

Specialization SHALL remain governed.

Override Transparency

Overrides SHALL always be explicit.

6. Canonical Inheritance Relationships

Atlas defines the following inheritance relationships.

Relationship	Description
Inherits	Acquires parent behavior
Specializes	Narrows parent applicability
Extends	Adds new behavior
Overrides	Replaces authorized behavior
Aggregates	Combines multiple policies
References	Uses another policy without inheritance

Relationships SHALL conform to ADS-011 — Relationship Model.

7. Inheritance Hierarchy

Atlas supports hierarchical inheritance.

Global Policy
      │
      ▼
Organization Policy
      │
      ▼
Domain Policy
      │
      ▼
Project Policy
      │
      ▼
Operational Policy

Lower levels SHALL inherit higher-level mandatory governance unless explicitly overridden by authorized authority.

8. Specialization

Specialization MAY:

Narrow applicability.
Add constraints.
Increase security.
Increase validation requirements.
Add obligations.
Add governance requirements.

Specialization SHALL NOT weaken mandatory parent requirements without an authorized override.

9. Extension

Extensions MAY introduce:

Additional conditions
Additional obligations
Additional recommendations
Additional metadata
Domain-specific vocabulary

Extensions SHALL preserve inherited semantics.

10. Override Rules

Overrides SHALL define:

Parent policy
Overridden element
Override justification
Override authority
Effective period
Approval reference

Overrides SHALL remain independently auditable.

11. Multiple Inheritance

Atlas permits controlled multiple inheritance.

Security Policy
        │
        ├────────┐
        ▼        ▼
Validation Policy
        │
        ▼
Project Policy

Multiple inheritance SHALL require conflict analysis before activation.

12. Conflict Prevention

Inheritance SHALL detect conflicts including:

Opposing effects
Duplicate obligations
Priority ambiguity
Scope overlap
Version incompatibility
Governance inconsistency

Conflicts SHALL prevent activation until resolved.

13. Inheritance Governance

Every inheritance relationship SHALL define:

Parent authority
Child authority
Applicable governance domains
Delegation rules
Review authority
Audit requirements

Governance SHALL remain independently traceable.

14. Inheritance Quality

Every inheritance hierarchy SHALL satisfy:

Consistency
Completeness
Traceability
Explainability
Reusability
Maintainability
Governance compliance
Conflict freedom

Quality SHALL be measurable.

15. Inheritance Lifecycle

Inheritance relationships SHALL support:

Defined
    │
Validated
    │
Approved
    │
Activated
    │
Modified
    │
Deprecated
    │
Retired

Inheritance history SHALL remain reconstructable.

16. Conformance

A Policy Inheritance implementation conforms if it:

Supports explicit inheritance.
Preserves mandatory parent semantics.
Supports governed specialization.
Detects inheritance conflicts.
Records inheritance lineage.
Maintains governance metadata.
Preserves traceability.
17. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Semantic inheritance analysis.
AI-assisted specialization.
Dynamic inheritance.
Cross-enterprise inheritance.
Ontology-driven inheritance.
Distributed policy hierarchies.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical inheritance semantics.

